<h3 id="battery-labeling">Battery Labeling</h3>
<p>Batteries usually have a label on them denoting how many cells such as 1S, 2S, 3S, 4S etc. So, why “S” and not “C” for “cells”? The “S” stands for “series” and it means that there are 2<strong>S</strong> or two cells in <strong>s</strong>eries. Cells in series add voltage while cells in parallel add capacity (mAh). The typical resting voltage for a LiPo cell is 3.7volts. Therefore a 2 cell battery in series, or, 2S, would have a voltage of 7.4 volts (3.7V x 2 = 7.4V). Here is a chart that shows cell count in series, resting voltage and charged voltage for common LiPo batteries. It is not advised to drain a battery below the “safe” voltage listed below as it will likely destroy the battery cell(s) and ruin the entire battery.</p>
