<h3 id="charging-a-battery">Charging a battery</h3>
<p>The Venom dual battery charger looks like this when you first power it on.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-charger-front.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-charger-front.jpg" alt="The front of the Venom charger with its blue screen lit, a red power light, and five buttons below the screen." width="480" height="640" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-charger-buttons.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-charger-buttons.jpg" alt="The Venom charger&#x27;s screen reading LiPo BALANCE 2.2A 22.2V(6S), above the Select/Stop, DEC, INC, and Start/Enter buttons and the Channel button below them." width="480" height="640" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<p>When you look at the front of the charger you will see a row of four buttons and a single &quot;channel&quot; button below. The “channel” button will switch between the two chargers that are built into this charger. An LED lights up to show you which side of the charger you are currently working with. This is the biggest area of confusion for people. When the charger seems to be acting strangely, it is usually because the wrong channel is selected. When the LED on the left side is lit up, all the controls are for the charging cable and balance cable on the left. When the right LED is on all of the controls are for the charging cable and balance cable on the right.</p>
<p>The top row of buttons, working left to right, consists of the following buttons (note, some buttons serve two roles)  Select/Stop, DEC (decrement) INC (increment) and a start/enter button.</p>
<p>You will need to connect your battery’s XT-60 (or other) power connector to the charger and the battery’s balance lead as shown below.</p>
<ul class="parts-grid">
<li><figure class="part-card"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/lipo-xt60-balance-lead.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/lipo-xt60-balance-lead.jpg" alt="A LiPo battery with its leads standing up; arrows label the yellow XT-60 power connector and the white balance lead." width="480" height="640" loading="lazy"></a></figure></li>
<li><figure class="part-card"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/balance-board-connection.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/balance-board-connection.jpg" alt="A hand plugging the battery&#x27;s balance lead into a balance board connected to the charger; the battery&#x27;s XT-60 lead connects to the charger&#x27;s red and black leads." width="480" height="640" loading="lazy"></a></figure></li>
</ul>
<p>Next, you will need to select the correct chemistry for the battery you are trying to charge. In this case, you have a LiPo battery. You will need to push the “select” button as shown below until the screen shows the LiPo setting as shown in the second photo.</p>
<ul class="parts-grid">
<li><figure class="part-card"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-program-select.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-program-select.jpg" alt="A finger pressing the Select button on the charger while the screen reads PROGRAM SELECT SAVE DATA." width="480" height="640" loading="lazy"></a></figure></li>
<li><figure class="part-card"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-program-select-lipo.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-program-select-lipo.jpg" alt="A finger pressing the Select button on the charger while the screen reads PROGRAM SELECT LiPo BATT." width="480" height="640" loading="lazy"></a></figure></li>
</ul>
<p>Then, press the “start/enter” button as shown below which will bring you into the sub menu shown below.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-lipo-charge.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-lipo-charge.jpg" alt="A finger pressing the INC button on the charger while the screen shows a LiPo CHARGE setting." width="480" height="640" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<p>Now, press the “INC” button until you see the “LiPo BALANCE” menu as shown below.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-lipo-balance.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-lipo-balance.jpg" alt="A finger pressing the INC button on the charger while the screen shows the LiPo BALANCE menu with its current and voltage settings." width="480" height="640" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<p>Next, press the start/enter button once and the current setting will begin to flash. You will need to set this to the appropriate current for the battery that you are trying to charge. While the current setting is flashing, you can press the “INC” button to increase the current and the “DEC” button to decrease the current. Remember, you divide the battery capacity by 1000 to determine the charge current. Therefore a 1500mAh battery should be charged at 1.5 amps or less.</p>
<p>Once you have the proper current selected press the “start/enter” button once and now the voltage should be flashing. Again, use the “INC” or “DEC” buttons to increase or decrease the voltage to match the voltage and cell count of the battery that you are trying to charge.</p>
<p>When you have the correct voltage and current selected press and hold the “start/enter” button until the confirmation screen appears, similar to the one below (although the text might be different depending on the size and type of battery you are trying to charge).</p>
<p>Then, release the “start/enter” button then press one more time to start the charging process.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-start-charge.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-start-charge.jpg" alt="A finger pressing the Start/Enter button on the charger while the screen shows the confirmation to start charging." width="480" height="640" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<p>If you did everything correctly your battery should now be charging. You can press the “INC” button to see the individual cells as the battery charges.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-cell-voltages.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/venom-cell-voltages.jpg" alt="A finger pressing INC on the charger while the screen shows the voltage of each cell, each about 3.7 to 3.8 volts." width="480" height="640" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<p>The charger will stop automatically when charging is complete.</p>
<h2 id="matching-the-battery-propeller-motor-and-esc">Matching the battery, propeller, motor, and ESC</h2>
<p>Now that you have learned about how batteries, motors, esc, and propellers work, we will combine them into a power system to generate thrust. It is important to correctly match these components so as not to exceed the safe working limits of any of the individual components.</p>
<p>Exceeding a safe operating current can cause damage to motors, ESCs, and batteries. For example, changing the voltage of the battery that powers a motor from a 3 cell to a 4 cell will increase the amount of current in the circuit. It would be important in this example, to make sure that the ESC can handle this extra current and that the discharge C rating of the battery won’t be exceeded. Similarly, changing the propeller on the motor from a 9 inch to a 10 inch propeller will cause the motor to draw more current. Again, it is important that this increased current will not exceed the ESC, motor, or battery current limits. It is important to understand that changing an ESC from a 15 amp ESC to a 30 amp ESC will NOT change the current draw in the circuit. The current rating on an ESC only states how much current the ESC can safely handle. A larger ESC does NOT “make” more current. Current is a product of voltage and resistance in a circuit. Current can also increase by increasing physical load on a motor. Load can be increased by changing to a longer propeller or changing the pitch of a propeller.</p>
<h2 id="control-horns">Control Horns</h2>
<p>A control horn is designed to change the direction of motion in a mechanical system. Control horns are used at the end of push rods to change the linear motion of the pushrod into rotational motion on the control surface. At the servo end of the push rod servo arms change the rotational motion of the servo into linear motion. The control horn pictured below is designed to be glued into the foamboard to secure it in place. A piece of stiff piano wire with a Z bend will form the push rod.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/control-horn-cad.png"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/control-horn-cad.png" alt="A CAD rendering of a control horn: a curved, tapered fin with a pushrod hole near its tip, standing on a T-shaped base that slots into the control surface." width="1320" height="796" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/control-horns-printed.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/control-horns-printed.jpg" alt="Two white 3D-printed control horns lying on a blue print bed." width="1600" height="1200" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<h2 id="pushrods">Pushrods</h2>
<p>Pushrods are used to provide a mechanical linkage between servos and control horns. Pictured below is a pushrod with a Z bend at one end.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/pushrod-z-bend.png"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/pushrod-z-bend.png" alt="A CAD rendering of a straight wire pushrod with a Z-shaped bend at one end." width="1320" height="796" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<p>This Z bend can be pushed into the hole on a servo arm or a control horn. The best way to connect servo arms to control horns is with a Z bend at each end. While there are linkage stoppers and other devices that make connecting servos and control horns easier, this method has the least points of failure and uses the least resources. While the Z bend in these piano wire pushrods can be made with pliers, a Z bend tool makes the job much easier.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/z-bend-pliers.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/z-bend-pliers.jpg" alt="Red-handled Z-bend pliers with stepped jaws, and an inset showing the jaws from another angle." width="769" height="772" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<h2 id="linkage-stopper">Linkage Stopper</h2>
<p>Pictured below is a linkage stopper. A linkage stopper bolts to either a control horn or servo arm. The pushrod passes through the large hole in the stopper and is secured in place with a set screw. The primary advantage of a linkage stopper is easy pushrod adjustment. By loosening the set screw, the pushrod can be moved in or out to mechanically center and trim the flight control surface. This allows the control surface to be properly aligned without relying on electronic trim adjustments in the transmitter. Although linkage stoppers are convenient and allow for very fine adjustments, they also introduce additional potential failure points. The set screw securing the pushrod can loosen, and the retaining nut on the threaded post can also work loose during operation. Either failure could disconnect or allow movement of the control linkage and potentially result in a loss of control in flight. For this reason, all linkage stopper hardware should be securely tightened and inspected before flight. A suitable thread-locking compound may also be used on the retaining nut where appropriate.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/linkage-stoppers.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/linkage-stoppers.jpg" alt="Ten nickel-plated linkage stoppers: small cylinders with a cross hole for the pushrod, a set screw, and a threaded post with a nut." width="679" height="560" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<p>A slightly better option than a linkage stopper is a pushrod with a Z-bend on one end and a threaded clip on the other. The Z-bend provides a simple and secure connection to the servo arm, while the threaded clip connects to the control horn. These pushrods tend to be very reliable and still provide some mechanical adjustment. The threaded end can be turned in or out to make small changes to the pushrod length, allowing the control surface to be mechanically centered without relying heavily on transmitter trims. These pushrods cost a little more than using basic piano wire, but they provide a strong, reliable, and easily adjustable control linkage and are a good choice for most RC aircraft.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/pushrods-with-clevises.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/pushrods-with-clevises.jpg" alt="Wire pushrods with Z-bends at one end and threaded metal clevises at the other, with white plastic pushrod tubes." width="595" height="304" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<p>When installing servos, pushrods and control horns, you want to make sure that the control surface is in its neutral position and that the servo is in its neutral position. You will not know if the servo is in its centered neutral position unless you power on the servo using the receiver, ESC, and a battery.</p>
<h2 id="tools">Tools</h2>
<h3 id="z-bend-pliers">Z-Bend Pliers</h3>
<p>These <strong>“Z” bend pliers</strong> are great for making “Z” bends in piano wire for pushrods. While you can make Z bends with regular pliers, this tool makes the process much easier with better quality.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/z-bend-pliers.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/z-bend-pliers.jpg" alt="Red-handled Z-bend pliers with stepped jaws, and an inset showing the jaws from another angle." width="769" height="772" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<h2 id="soldering-and-crimping">Soldering and Crimping</h2>
<p>Soldering is a basic skill that is very important in the remote aircraft world. Knowing how to solder correctly and how to solder well will pay dividends as you progress in the unmanned aircraft world. There are a variety of tools that you can use to solder however the most basic setup requires only a soldering iron, sponge, flux, and solder. Brass wool, a rework tool, microscope and other tools are nice to have but are not required. Soldering involves melting a filler material to join two pieces of metal (usually copper) together to form a mechanical as well as an electrical connection.</p>
<p>Solder is a metal made up of lead and tin. Lead free options are available that contain tin, with added elements like copper, silver, bismuth, zinc, antimony, and nickel. Solder melts at a range of temperatures, typically between 180°C (360°F) and 220°C (430°F), depending on the specific alloy composition. Leaded solder, like 60/40 (60% tin, 40% lead), melts around 183°C (361°F), while lead-free solder alloys tend to have a slightly higher melting point, often around 217°C (423°F) for common lead-free solder.</p>
<h3 id="solder-station">Solder Station</h3>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/solder-station.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/solder-station.jpg" alt="A Weller soldering station: a red base with a temperature dial, a coiled iron holder, a yellow cleaning sponge, and a soldering iron on a cord." width="658" height="651" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<h3 id="solder-wool">Solder wool</h3>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/solder-wool.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/solder-wool.jpg" alt="A round black holder filled with brass solder wool for cleaning the soldering iron tip." width="894" height="894" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<h3 id="solder">Solder</h3>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/solder-spool.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/solder-spool.jpg" alt="A spool of 0.6 mm lead-free RoHS solder wire, 99.3% tin and 0.7% bismuth, with a 2.5% flux core." width="952" height="822" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<h3 id="solder-wick">Solder Wick</h3>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/uas-hardware/solder-wick.jpg"><img src="/assets/uas-hardware/solder-wick.jpg" alt="A spool of braided copper desoldering wick, 10 feet long and 0.1 inch wide." width="1500" height="1426" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
<h2 id="how-to-solder">How to Solder</h2>
<p>Knowing how to solder is a valuable skill. When working with remote aircraft the ability to solder will allow you to build and repair a multitude of electrical systems. You do not need a fancy expensive soldering station, but at a minimum a soldering iron with a heat control, brass wool, wet sponge, flux, and solder will do most of what you need. A better, more expensive setup will make your work easier, but, for most day to day soldering, a simple setup will be just fine.</p>
<p>You want to make sure that your soldering iron is clean. Keep it clean by dipping it in and out of the brass wool and wiping the tip on a damp sponge. Don’t try to use any solvents other than flux and never sand or file the tip. Clean the tip often while working. The tip of the iron should always be clean and shiny. The tip will oxidize quickly (in seconds) so frequent cleaning will be a must for success.</p>
<p>The iron works best with a small amount of fresh solder on the tip before attempting to make a connection. A few drops of liquid flux make a night and day difference in your ability to solder good clean joints. Make sure that you know the type of flux you are using and how it will be cleaned off after you are done. Water soluble flux is recommended as you can easily wash the flux off with soap and water. Make sure that parts that have been washed have adequate time to dry before powering on. Letting freshly washed parts dry overnight is a good safe practice.</p>
<p>The contacts that you are trying to join need to melt the solder not the tip of your iron. The iron provides the heat to heat the connections but the connections themselves should melt the solder. A good solder joint will be shiny and silver, not dull and gray. A good solder joint should not have any black specks in it either. Any discoloration represents impurities which mean a poor connection and a possible failure point. Clean and re-solder any bad joints.</p>
<h3 id="desoldering-a-connection">Desoldering a connection</h3>
<p>If you need to undo a solder connection there are a few options depending on how the connection was made and how difficult it is to heat and remove the component without causing damage to other parts of the circuit board or electric connection. The main tools required are solder wick, solder pump (solder sucker), flux, solder iron and/or a rework tool. Regardless of the tools used, the goal is to melt the solder and remove it in order to free the component or wire that you are trying to unsolder. Flux is your friend when trying to desolder something. Flux will help the solder to melt and flow better. Add a few drops of flux to a piece of solder wick then lay the solder wick over the connection you want to remove. Then press the iron onto the wick and allow it to melt and absorb the solder. The solder wick should suck up the melted solder like a sponge. Typically this will remove most but not all of the solder. You may find that you may need to apply a little additional heat then remove the component with tweezers or needle nose pliers applying gentle force.</p>
